1a installation of wsl linux was a relatively easy process, felt a little bit nervous to add a subsystem to my laptop as I was not sure how it would have affected my laptop.
learnt to add a user01 and add password
familiarised the wsl linux with a few commands from the lab exercises
commands used:
ps -e, followed by top, which showed the cpu threads and the usage of the memory
ls, followed by ls -la, the difference between these 2 commands are how the files are shown, with ls -la showing user01 while the other does not
sudo apt install gedit, gedit testfile, nano testfile and touch testfile, the difference between the 2 would be gedit opens a window to edit the file while nano does not
cat testfile and less testfile, the difference being how the content is displayed
cp and mv testfile, where it felt like there was no visible difference between the 2.
uname -a and lsb_release -a, where both showed different information such as ditributor id, descriptiopon , etc
hostnamectl, showing information of the host such as static hostname, chassis, os, architecture, etc
whoami and add user and  whoami, which asks the wsl which user is currently using, adding another user and adding another user as a root user

sudo apt update updates the wsl
sudo apt install nginx-full installs the nginx webserver
sudo apt install nmap and sudo apt install openssh-server was relatively easy to install
